I am learning and playing around with torch and was implementing custom feed forward layer to my model when I encountered Error: Error in x$shape: object of type 'closure' is not subsettable. Here is small reprex from the issue: 

``` r
library(torch)
#> Warning: package 'torch' was built under R version 4.2.3

data <- torch::torch_randn(1,6000,128)

FeedForwardLayer <- torch::nn_module(
  classname = "FeedForwardLayer",
  initialize = function(d_model, d_ff=128, dropout=0.1) {
    
    self$linear_1 <- torch::nn_linear(d_model, d_ff)
    self$dropout <- torch::nn_dropout(dropout)
    self$linear_2 <- torch::nn_linear(d_ff, d_model)
  },
  
  forward = function(x) {
    x <- self$dropout$forward(torch::nnf_relu(self$linear_1$forward(x)))
    x <- self$linear_2$forward(x)
    return(x)
  }
  
)

layers <- torch::nn_module_list(
  lapply(seq_len(2), function(x) {
    torch::nn_sequential(FeedForwardLayer$new(d_model=128))
  })
)

layers[[1]]$forward(data)
#> Error in x$shape: object of type 'closure' is not subsettable
```

I noticed that when I jump to nn_sequential forward function and replace,
``` r
for (module in private$modules_) {
      input <- module(input)
    }
    input
```
With,
``` r
for (module in private$modules_) {
      input <- module$forward(input)
    }
    input
```
The error does not occur and calculations are done correctly. Any ideas what's going on?

I expected `dataset_subset` to keep dataset properties, but it hides them. Could we either sort out a way to elevate the dataset to the "top" of that object, or at least give the new dataset a subclass so methods can know to look down a level for "real" dataset properties?

``` r
library(torch)
minimal_dataset <- dataset(
  "minimal",
  initialize = function() {
    self$data <- torch_tensor(1:5)
  },
  .length = function() {
    self$data$size()[[1]]
  }
)
testing <- minimal_dataset()
class(testing)
#> [1] "minimal" "dataset" "R6"
testing_sub <- dataset_subset(testing, 1:2)
class(testing_sub)
#> [1] "dataset" "R6"
```

